## What to change first

`game/game.go` holds a placeholder game: two slots take turns claiming
cells on a small board, and the slot holding the most cells at the end
wins. It exists so the loop around your rules already runs. Replace it a
piece at a time:

| Piece | What it decides |
|---|---|
| `State` | the world; everything the session commits |
| `Action` | what one decision is |
| `Observation` | what one slot may see — narrow this before shipping |
| `Game.ActingSlots` | who decides this step |
| `Game.Apply` | how a validated action advances the world |
| `Game.Project` | how the world becomes an observation |
| `Game.Evaluate` | the score, progress, and terminal state of a slot |
| `Validator.Legal` | which actions are possible at all |

`Observation` is the one worth attention early. It is a separate type
from `State` even though the placeholder copies the whole board into it,
because narrowing what a slot can see later is a change to `Project`
alone — and every agent, human or not, reads only its observation.

## Configuration

`ebigent.toml` holds both the toolchain settings and the run settings.
Anything in it can be overridden by an environment variable or a command
line option, in that order:

    RUN_TOPOLOGY=dedicated go run ./cmd/{{(index .Targets 0).Name}}
    go run ./cmd/{{(index .Targets 0).Name}} --run-topology dedicated

Under `ebigent`, a configuration option goes before the verb, because the
verb consumes everything after its own name:

    ebigent --run-topology dedicated build {{(index .Targets 0).Name}}

## Recording

`corpus/` and `behavior/chips.json` are already in place. Recording is
off until `[run] episode.dir` names a directory — turn it on before you
need the data, because a corpus cannot be collected retroactively.
